+### Durations
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a string such as `90s`, `1h30m`, or `1m500ms` to a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. A
+bare integer is a number of seconds, so `90` means `90s`. The units are `d`, `h`, `m`, `s`, and `ms`. They must appear
+in that order, and each can be used at most once. Negative durations are rejected.
+
+`min` and `max` bound the accepted durations. Both bounds are inclusive, and either can be omitted.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    from datetime import timedelta
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--wait", type=click.Duration(min=timedelta(seconds=1), max=timedelta(hours=1)))
+    def pause(wait):
+        click.echo(wait)
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(pause, args=['--wait=1m30s'])
+    invoke(pause, args=['--wait=2h'])
+```
+
